WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:07.821
A bike shop in Utrecht changes its name, from Fietsen Utrecht to Velo Utrecht.

2
00:00:07.821 --> 00:00:11.695
The save deletes the cached copy right after, as it should.

3
00:00:11.900 --> 00:00:15.823
An hour later, a customer's receipt still says Fietsen Utrecht.

4
00:00:15.823 --> 00:00:19.871
If every save deletes the copy, how did the old name get back in?

5
00:00:20.067 --> 00:00:22.608
The rule from the last lesson is simple.

6
00:00:22.608 --> 00:00:26.800
Commit the new row to the database, then delete the copy in Redis.

7
00:00:26.967 --> 00:00:31.182
The next reader misses, loads the new row, and fills the cache.

8
00:00:31.182 --> 00:00:32.185
So far so good.

9
00:00:32.367 --> 00:00:33.738
Picture a shop window.

10
00:00:33.738 --> 00:00:39.473
The prices live on a list in the back office, and clerks copy them onto tags for the window.

11
00:00:39.473 --> 00:00:45.208
When a price changes, someone takes the old tag down, and the next clerk copies a fresh one.

12
00:00:45.333 --> 00:00:47.113
The list is the database.

13
00:00:47.113 --> 00:00:48.536
The window is Redis.

14
00:00:48.536 --> 00:00:52.735
A tag is the cached copy, and taking it down is the delete.

15
00:00:52.867 --> 00:00:54.576
Now picture a slow clerk.

16
00:00:54.576 --> 00:00:58.747
Start again: version 1 in the database, and nothing in Redis.

17
00:00:58.933 --> 00:01:03.338
Reader A, a server checking a payment, misses on the shop's key.

18
00:01:03.338 --> 00:01:06.435
It reads the row: version 1, Fietsen Utrecht.

19
00:01:06.633 --> 00:01:13.232
Then it stalls before it can store it: a pause to clean up memory, or a wait for a database connection.

20
00:01:13.433 --> 00:01:17.556
Meanwhile, the writer renames the shop and commits version 2.

21
00:01:17.556 --> 00:01:19.246
Then it deletes the copy.

22
00:01:19.246 --> 00:01:24.248
But the window is empty, so the delete removes nothing: zero keys deleted.

23
00:01:24.467 --> 00:01:29.572
Now reader A wakes up and sends its SET: Fietsen Utrecht, for an hour.

24
00:01:29.572 --> 00:01:30.957
Will Redis take it?

25
00:01:30.957 --> 00:01:32.124
Pause and guess.

26
00:01:35.333 --> 00:01:35.892
It does.

27
00:01:35.892 --> 00:01:42.323
Redis keeps no record of the delete, and no versions, so it stores the old name for an hour.

28
00:01:42.500 --> 00:01:46.134
Reader B, and every reader after it, gets Fietsen Utrecht.

29
00:01:46.134 --> 00:01:51.772
And no save is coming to delete it: the save that should have deleted it was already over.

30
00:01:51.967 --> 00:01:54.810
Storing a copy after a miss is called a fill.

31
00:01:54.810 --> 00:02:00.181
A fill that stores a value already out of date when it arrives is called a stale set.

32
00:02:00.181 --> 00:02:05.299
Meta, the company behind Facebook, named it in a paper about its cache, memcache.

33
00:02:05.500 --> 00:02:11.779
The race needs two things: A's read comes before the commit, and A's fill comes after the delete.

34
00:02:11.967 --> 00:02:15.978
Between a read and its fill there's usually a millisecond or two.

35
00:02:15.978 --> 00:02:18.138
A pause can stretch it to hundreds.

36
00:02:18.138 --> 00:02:21.779
So it's rare for one request, and frequent across millions.

37
00:02:21.967 --> 00:02:27.742
When Meta measured its own cache, about one write in a million was still wrong five minutes later.

38
00:02:27.742 --> 00:02:31.985
One in a million sounds safe, until you serve a quadrillion reads a day.

39
00:02:32.167 --> 00:02:35.416
At that volume, it's a steady stream of wrong answers.

40
00:02:35.700 --> 00:02:38.195
Here the TTL caps the damage at an hour.

41
00:02:38.195 --> 00:02:40.504
For a shop's name, that's a nuisance.

42
00:02:40.504 --> 00:02:47.304
For a flag that says the shop is suspended for fraud, it's an hour of payments that should have been refused.

43
00:02:47.500 --> 00:02:51.041
The tempting fix is to delete the key again, a moment later.

44
00:02:51.041 --> 00:02:54.404
It helps, since most slow readers finish within the wait.

45
00:02:54.600 --> 00:02:57.267
But a reader that pauses longer still gets in.

46
00:02:57.267 --> 00:03:02.891
It shrinks the gap without closing it, and you have to guess how slow your slowest reader can be.

47
00:03:03.100 --> 00:03:04.275
Back to the window.

48
00:03:04.275 --> 00:03:08.853
Before a clerk copies a price, he takes a numbered ticket from the office.

49
00:03:08.853 --> 00:03:16.091
A price change tears up every ticket for that item, and the window takes a tag only with a ticket that's still valid.

50
00:03:16.267 --> 00:03:18.646
That ticket is called a lease.

51
00:03:18.646 --> 00:03:25.069
In Redis, the reader takes it on a miss: SET lease, a random token, NX, PX 10000.

52
00:03:25.267 --> 00:03:33.252
NX means only if nobody holds it, and PX 10000 lets it vanish after ten seconds if the reader crashes.

53
00:03:33.467 --> 00:03:38.376
After its commit, the writer deletes the copy and the lease, in one command.

54
00:03:38.376 --> 00:03:46.451
And the fill runs as a small script inside Redis: if the lease still holds my token, store the name, otherwise store nothing.

55
00:03:46.667 --> 00:03:51.900
A script runs as one command, so no delete can land between the check and the store.

56
00:03:52.133 --> 00:03:53.319
Replay the race.

57
00:03:53.319 --> 00:03:57.395
A misses, takes lease t-A, reads version 1, and stalls.

58
00:03:57.600 --> 00:04:02.812
The writer commits version 2 and deletes both keys: one key deleted, the lease.

59
00:04:03.033 --> 00:04:06.637
A wakes up, and its fill is refused: the lease is gone.

60
00:04:06.637 --> 00:04:09.127
The old name never reaches the window.

61
00:04:09.300 --> 00:04:14.768
Reader B misses, takes lease t-B, reads version 2, and fills Velo Utrecht.

62
00:04:15.233 --> 00:04:15.911
Two notes.

63
00:04:15.911 --> 00:04:23.496
A lease works only when every fill starts with a miss; the lesson's next fix, a version number, covers the rest.

64
00:04:23.496 --> 00:04:27.221
And keep the TTL: it caps whatever still slips through.

65
00:04:27.400 --> 00:04:35.200
In Java and Kotlin, the reader takes the lease with SET, NX and PX, and fills with one EVAL of the script.

66
00:04:35.200 --> 00:04:38.144
The writer sends one DEL with both keys.

67
00:04:38.833 --> 00:04:41.038
So how did the old name get back in?

68
00:04:41.038 --> 00:04:43.732
A slow reader put it there after the delete.

69
00:04:43.933 --> 00:04:51.471
On a busy system, things don't happen in the order your code lists them, so a late copy must show a ticket that's still valid.

70
00:04:51.867 --> 00:04:52.971
One quick check.

71
00:04:52.971 --> 00:04:58.702
Reader A takes the lease and stalls for three seconds, but nobody renames the shop.

72
00:04:58.702 --> 00:05:00.773
Is A's fill stored or refused?

73
00:05:00.773 --> 00:05:01.877
Pause and think.

74
00:05:05.067 --> 00:05:05.558
Stored.

75
00:05:05.558 --> 00:05:09.489
Nothing tore up its ticket, and version 1 is still true.

76
00:05:09.489 --> 00:05:13.279
A lease refuses only copies that a write has made old.
